Phase 1 — GTO Chart Data Layer

Research and source preflop GTO charts for each position (RFI ranges, 3bet ranges, calling ranges) — sites like GTOWizard or Poker Coach have these
Encode them as Python data structures (dicts or JSON files), keyed by position and hand (e.g. "BTN": {"AKs": "raise", "72o": "fold", ...})
Build a gto.py module with a lookup(position, hand) -> action function
You'll need a hand normalizer to convert your Card objects into a standard notation like "AKs" or "72o"


Phase 2 — Refactor to Preflop-Only Mode

Strip out or gate-off the flop/turn/river logic for now — your trainer only cares about preflop
Simplify gameStart() to: deal hands → post blinds → run one preflop betting round → evaluate Hero's action → give feedback


Phase 3 — Hero Feedback System

After Hero acts, compare their action to gto.lookup(hero.position, hero.hand)
Print feedback: correct / incorrect, and what the GTO play would have been
Optionally show the frequency (e.g. "GTO raises this hand 70% of the time")


Phase 4 — AI Villain GTO Actions

Replace the random/call-only villain logic in get_villain_action() with a GTO lookup
Villain raises, calls, or folds based on their position's chart
This makes the training environment more realistic


Phase 5 — Loop & Session Tracking

Wrap gameStart() in a loop so Hero can play many hands in a row
Track a simple score: correct actions / total hands
Print a summary at the end of the session


Key new files you'll need:

gto.py — chart data + lookup logic
hand_parser.py — converts your Card objects to chart notation (AKs, T9o, etc.)
Modify: betting.py, board.py (your main file), and optionally deckclass.py